22:36:51 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a fantastic point, and that documentary likely highlighted a real practice that *does* happen—but it's the gold-plated exception, not the everyday rule. It doesn’t disprove the problem; it actually reveals exactly why so many films *don't* get the permanent treatment. When a studio does a high-profile restoration and "locks in all the rights for good," what they're really doing is this: ### The Perpetual, All-Media, Worldwide Buyout Instead of the patchwork license I described earlier, the studio goes back to every single music rights holder and negotiates a new, turbo-charged license that says, in essence: *“We are paying you a very large, lump-sum fee for the right to use this song in this specific film, in any and all formats, in every territory, for the entire duration of the copyright.”* This is often called a **“buyout in perpetuity.”** It’s the closest thing to actually “owning” the song for that film’s context without buying the copyright itself. So yes, you’re right: **permanence is technically possible.** But the reason it’s not the norm comes down to brutal economics and logistics: **1. The cost is often astronomical.** For a beloved classic, a single popular song can cost hundreds of thousands of dollars for a perpetual, all-media license. Multiply that by a soundtrack with 20 or 30 songs, and you’ve just added millions to the restoration budget. Unless the film is a guaranteed cash cow (think *The Godfather* or *Star Wars*), the studio isn't going to spend $3 million to clear music for a film that might only generate $2 million in re-release profit. They’ll just swap the songs. **2. You can’t always find (or agree with) all the rights holders.** Music rights are notoriously tangled. A single song might have a half-dozen co-writers, multiple publishers split by territory, and an underlying sample that needs its own clearance. Even with a blank check, you might not get everyone to say yes. One recalcitrant publisher in G